From 79c44155e16920ab19d31e7f1df628b9c4d81d93 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: atomozero <bernardi.andrea@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:47:34 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 34/42] tegra20_cpufreq: CPU clock and voltage of the Tegra 2
 together

A cpufreq module for the NVIDIA Tegra 2: it runs the CPUs at one of the
operating points of Linux' tegra20-cpu-opp.dtsi, from 216 to 1000 MHz,
each at the lowest CPU voltage that the fuses of the chip allow (CPU
process and SoC speedo ID, read as Linux' speedo-tegra20.c does). Raising
the rate raises the CPU supply (SM1 of the TPS6586x) first, lowering it
lowers the supply after; the core supply must stay 120 mV above. The
clock part is the one tegra_dvfs had, which now asks the module through
"cclk <MHz>" and shows its state.

For now the rate is only chosen by hand: the scheduler's hooks note
nothing yet, a governor comes next. "disabled true" in the tegra20_cpufreq
driver settings keeps the module from loading.

The kernel now reports the rate the module set (arm_set_cpu_frequency()),
not the one it measured once at boot. A reset of the chip leaves the
power management IC as it is while the boot firmware starts at full
speed: before resetting, the kernel raises the supplies to their nominal
voltages again (arm_tegra_prepare_reset()), as Linux does.

Tested on the Toshiba AC100 (process 1, speedo ID 0: 216/750, 312/750,
456/825, 608/900, 760/975, 816/1000, 912/1050, 1000/1100 MHz/mV):
tests/ac100diag/cpucheck, the same integer and floating point work on
both CPUs checked against its first result, 20 s at every point going
down without an error; tests/ac100diag/clocktest, 27 changes with jumps
of up to 350 mV, timing kept. On battery, idle with the screen on:
4.66 W at 1000 MHz, 4.31 W at 456 MHz, 4.23 W at 216 MHz.

Signed-off-by: atomozero <bernardi.andrea@gmail.com>
